---
name: dev-task
version: 3.10.0
description: lead dev assignment for a planned dev bead in atm-bd-orchestration. The bead is the assignment; task_id is the bead id.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - stack
  - pr_target
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - primary_checkout
  - phase_root
  - test_command
  - policy_path
metadata:
  type: dev-task
  tags:
    - stage:dev
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: dev-start
    stage: dev
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}">
  <description><![CDATA[Implement dev bead `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (sprint {{ sprint | string | cdata_escape }}). `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is the assignment: its description, design, acceptance criteria and metadata are authoritative.]]></description>

  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>
  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <stack><![CDATA[{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}]]></stack>
  <pr-target><![CDATA[{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}]]></pr-target>
  <stack-discipline><![CDATA[`{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` is cut from `origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`, the top of stack `{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}` at dispatch; its PR lands on the stack's current top. Work only on this branch. The task assigner is the stack writer: you never run `gh stack link`, `sync`, `rebase` or `merge`. Once linked the layer is frozen; later changes are fix beads.]]></stack-discipline>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Before claiming, run `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` in the worktree (a conflict is part of the fix), then `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py dev --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} --worktree {{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}` from the worktree (scripts come from the primary checkout, never the branch). Each check, its command and its refusal code: (1) plan valid: `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-beads/scripts/validate-plan --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0, else `PLAN_INVALID`; (2) ready: `bd ready -n 0 --json` lists the bead, else `NOT_READY`; (3) claimable without `--force`: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --json` shows status `open` and assignee empty or `$ATM_IDENTITY`, else `UNCLAIMABLE`; (4) target: `metadata.pr_target` is `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` or `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/<metadata.pr_target> origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0 (`pr_target` is a lower bound), else `PR_TARGET_MISMATCH`; (5) difficulty: your `atm members --json` model is in `DIFFICULTY_MODELS[metadata.difficulty]`, else `DIFFICULTY_MISMATCH`; (6) base: `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} HEAD` exits 0, else `WRONG_BASE`. `GATE_CANNOT_RUN` when a command fails. Reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; do not create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. On success claim with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --claim --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`, then start `atm task start {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`.]]></step>
    <step id="a1"><![CDATA[When it prints `NOT_READY` the bead is not ready: do not claim it and do not start the task. Find the root cause: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (status, assignee), `bd blocked --json | jq --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} '.[] | select(.id == $b)'` (its open blockers), and `bd show <blocker>` for each (status, assignee, and what it is itself waiting on). Report it to the task assigner: the bead, why it is not ready, and which bead or agent has to move. Then wait; the task assigner re-dispatches or cancels the task.]]></step>
    <step id="c"><![CDATA[Read `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` before coding. Treat it as the authoritative source for scope, deliverables, deletion targets, validation, and acceptance criteria; `metadata.owned_paths` is your file fence. A field the bead lacks (a poured dev bead's deliverables, `owned_paths`, `requirements`, `adrs`) comes from its sprint container: `bd show <metadata.sprint_bead>`. If this assignment and the bead differ, the bead wins and the mismatch must be reported. Then read every id in `metadata.requirements` and `metadata.adrs` in the governing documents before coding: your change must satisfy each one, and QA checks it against them. `NONE` means the plan declares none; if the work turns out to touch a requirement or ADR anyway, report it to the task assigner before coding past it.]]></step>
    <step id="c0"><![CDATA[Read the repository QA policy `{{ policy_path | string | cdata_escape }}` before editing, and every guideline it names for the languages this task touches; the reviewers score against them.]]></step>
    <step id="d"><![CDATA[Work from the bead's deliverables and acceptance criteria; nothing outside its scope. Read the complete plan and enumerate all tasks that must be completed in the primary and any child beads; create an itemized private checklist outside the tracked tree with every task identified, and work through the checklist one item at a time. Commit on `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` as items land and push early (`git push -u origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` first) so the work is never only local. A push is not a close and needs no report.]]></step>
    <step id="d1"><![CDATA[If the work needs a change other live branches need at the same time (a shared type or trait signature, or a critical bug every branch carries), stop that edit and tell the task assigner the exact change and the branches it breaks; follow the assigner's base (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md`, Parallel Quick Fix) and report a quick-fix PR you open by plain `atm send`.]]></step>
    <step id="e"><![CDATA[Finish every item, then go through the checklist again one item at a time and confirm each is fully met; do not weaken a test, skip a criterion, or leave a placeholder. Run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}` and the bead's validation commands until they pass with zero failures.]]></step>
    <step id="f"><![CDATA[Rebase onto the stack's current top (it may have moved since dispatch): `git fetch origin`, the top is what `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py stack-top --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` prints on exit 0 (from GitHub's stacks); any other exit prints a code (`STACK_AMBIGUOUS`, `GATE_CANNOT_RUN`): never guess, refuse with it by step g1; then `git rebase origin/<top>`, re-run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}`, then `git push --force-with-lease origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`. Then open the PR against that top: `gh pr create --base <top> --head {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }} --fill` (never `--draft`), run `/sc-gh-stack-view` (read-only) and keep its output verbatim. Record the full head SHA, the top as `rebased_onto`, and the PR number and url.]]></step>
    <step id="g"><![CDATA[Close the bead and the task together: `bd close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --reason "dev complete at <short sha>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} completed --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/dev-complete.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/dev-complete-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. Build the vars from that template's `required_variables`: `task_id` and `sprint` unchanged from this assignment, `commit` the pushed head, `rebased_onto` the top, `pr_number` and `pr_url` from step f (the task assigner reads them from this close), `stack_view` the verbatim `/sc-gh-stack-view` output, the deliverable inventory, and the exact gate output lines; for deletion or cleanup work, add the before/after targeted symbol or code-path inventory as `inventory_md`. Write the vars file outside the repository and never commit it. Only this close frees your queue: the next assignment stays queued and never starts until `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is closed, and a plain `atm send` report does not close it.]]></step>
    <step id="g1"><![CDATA[If the bead cannot be completed at all, reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; do not create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. A serious infrastructure failure is also announced once per cause to the oversight recipients (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md` Lead Role). Declare failure with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status blocked --assignee "" --append-notes "DEV_CANNOT_COMPLETE; workflow class or escalation <reference>: <reason>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` (never `bd close`: a closed dev bead releases its sanity check) and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. A deliverable you could not finish is a failure, not a completion.]]></step>
    <step id="h"><![CDATA[After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
